{"name":"Silver Dagger","hex":"#c1c1d1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"dagger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A stabbing weapon, similar to a sword but with a short, double-edged blade.","The typographical mark †."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pierce with a dagger; to stab.","To perform the daggering dance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dagger"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Dagger (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Silver Dagger\", with variants such as \"Katy Dear\", \"Molly Dear\", \"The Green Fields and Meadows\", \"Awake, Awake, Ye Drowsy Sleepers\" and others, is an American folk ballad, whose origins lie possibly in Britain. These songs of different titles are closely related, and two strands in particular became popular in commercial country music and folk music recordings of the twentieth century: the \"Silver Dagger\" version popularised by Joan Baez, and the \"Katy Dear\" versions popularised by close harmony brother duets such as The Callahan Brothers, The Blue Sky Boys and The Louvin Brothers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_Dagger_melody.png/330px-Silver_Dagger_melody.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Dagger_(song)","wikidata":"Q2841418"},{"title":"Gold Dagger","blocks":[{"text":"The CWA Gold Dagger is an award given annually by the Crime Writers' Association of the United Kingdom since 1960 for the best crime novel of the year."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Dagger","wikidata":"Q1157223"},{"title":"Dagger","blocks":[{"text":"A dagger is a fighting knife typically with a very sharp point and usually one or two sharp edges, typically designed or capable of being used as a cutting or thrusting weapon. Daggers have been used throughout human history for close combat confrontations, and many cultures have used adorned daggers in ritual and ceremonial contexts. The dagger's distinctive shape and historic usage have made it iconic and symbolic. In the modern sense, a dagger is designed for close-proximity combat or self-defense; due to its use in historic weapon assemblages, it has associations with assassination and murder. However, double-edged knives play different roles in different social contexts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Fs-dagger.jpg/330px-Fs-dagger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dagger","wikidata":"Q182780"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg/500px-Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg/1280px-Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg/500px-Dagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bronze dagger from Lorestan, Iran, 2600–2350 BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADagger_Iran_Luristan_2600-2350_BCE_Bronze.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg/500px-Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg/1280px-Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg/500px-Poignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Neolithic dagger from the Muséum de Toulouse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoignard_0.188.1_2_fond.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Pu%C3%B1al_%C3%ADbero_de_front%C3%B3n_%28M.A.N.%29_01.jpg/500px-Pu%C3%B1al_%C3%ADbero_de_front%C3%B3n_%28M.A.N.%29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Pu%C3%B1al_%C3%ADbero_de_front%C3%B3n_%28M.A.N.%29_01.jpg/500px-Pu%C3%B1al_%C3%ADbero_de_front%C3%B3n_%28M.A.N.%29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pre-Roman Iberian iron dagger forged between the middle of the 5th and the 3rd century BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APu%C3%B1al_%C3%ADbero_de_f